\chapter{Kinematics}
Motion along a straight line is described by position $x(t)$, velocity
$v(t) = \dot{x}(t)$, and acceleration $a(t) = \dot{v}(t)$.
For constant acceleration $a$, integrating twice with initial velocity
$v_0$ and initial position $x_0$ gives
\begin{equation}
v(t) = v_0 + a t, \qquad x(t) = x_0 + v_0 t + \frac{1}{2} a t^2.
\end{equation}
Eliminating $t$ between the two expressions yields the time-independent
relation
\begin{equation}
v^2 = v_0^2 + 2 a \, (x - x_0),
\end{equation}
which is often the fastest route to the speed at a given position.
\chapter{Newtonian Dynamics}
Newton's second law states that the net force on a particle equals the
time rate of change of its momentum.
For constant mass this reduces to the familiar form
\begin{equation}
\vec{F}_{\mathrm{net}} = m \, \vec{a}.
\end{equation}
A block sliding down a frictionless incline of angle $\theta$
experiences only gravity and the normal force; resolving gravity along
the incline gives the acceleration directly,
\begin{equation}
a = g \sin\theta,
\end{equation}
which is independent of the mass of the block.
\chapter{Energy Methods}
The work-energy theorem equates the net work done on a particle with
the change in its kinetic energy,
\begin{equation}
W_{\mathrm{net}} = \frac{1}{2} m v^2 - \frac{1}{2} m v_0^2.
\end{equation}
Consider a particle of mass $m$ released from rest at height $h$.
Gravity is the only force doing work, so the theorem gives
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{2} m v^2 = m g h.
\end{equation}
Dividing by $m$ and taking the square root yields the speed at the
base,
\begin{equation}
v = \sqrt{2 g h}.
\end{equation}
The same result follows from the conservation of mechanical energy:
the sum of kinetic and potential energy is constant along the motion,
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{2} m v^2 + m g y = m g h,
\end{equation}
where $y$ is the height above the base, and setting $y = 0$ recovers
the expression above.
